##
   The trap. `self: mut Counter` reads as though the receiver is mutable, and
   `mut` on the *type* is what makes `self.n = ...` legal at all -- but structs
   are passed BY VALUE, so the write lands on a local copy and the caller's
   object is untouched.

   In the MIR: `Counter_bump` allocates its own `%self.addr` from the
   by-value parameter and stores into that. Nothing writes back through the
   caller's slot, so `c.n` is still 5 after the call. `bumped_value_only`
   returns the method's 6 and discards it.

   To actually mutate the caller you need a pointer receiver -- see
   pointer_receiver_writes_through.unn.
##
struct Counter { n: i32 }

impl Counter {
  func bump(self: mut Counter): i32 {
    self.n = self.n + 1i32;
    return self.n;
  }
}

func bumped_value_only(): i32 {
  var c: mut Counter = .Counter{.n = 5i32};
  var got = c.bump();
  return got;
}
